  "theory": {
    "intro": "The **reverse chain rule** undoes the chain rule for a power of a linear expression. For $a\\ne0$ and $n\\ne-1$, $\\int(ax+b)^n\\,dx=\\dfrac{(ax+b)^{n+1}}{a(n+1)}+C$. Increase the power by one and divide by the product of the new power and the coefficient of $x$.",
    "facts": [
"The linear expression $ax+b$ inside the bracket stays unchanged.",
"Increase the power of the bracket by one and divide by the new power, leaving the bracket itself unchanged.",
"Divide by the coefficient $a$ of $x$ as well as by the new power; this compensates for the factor introduced by the chain rule.",
"An outside constant multiplies the primitive: $\\int k(ax+b)^n\\,dx=\\dfrac{k(ax+b)^{n+1}}{a(n+1)}+C$.",
"The rule requires $a\\ne0$ and $n\\ne-1$, and applies on intervals where the integrand is real and continuous.",
"Write roots as fractional powers and reciprocal powers as negative powers before finding the primitive.",
"Check any answer by differentiating it with the chain rule.",
"For a definite integral, first check that the integrand is continuous on the whole integration interval. Then evaluate $[F(x)]_u^v=F(v)-F(u)$ using the original limits, without adding $+C$."
],
    "steps": [
"Write the function as a constant multiple of $(ax+b)^n$, using a fractional or negative power where needed.",
"Increase the power of the bracket by one, leaving the bracket unchanged.",
"Divide by the product of the new power and the coefficient of $x$, and retain any outside constant.",
"Add $+C$ for a general primitive. For a definite integral with a continuous integrand on the whole interval, substitute the limits and subtract the lower-limit value from the upper-limit value."
]
  },
